\section{Separate Meaning, Execution, and Deliverable}

An alternative execution model changes what authorizes work. A control-flow machine advances a program counter; a dataflow machine may fire an operation when operands are available; a synchronous machine computes one logical reaction; an eductive evaluator answers a demand for a value at a context. These models can describe overlapping computations, but they do not imply the same runtime mechanism. A compiler may sequentialize a dataflow graph, distribute actors, synthesize hardware, or interpret demands without changing the source model's denotation.

The output artifact must therefore be named precisely. A frontend can produce an annotated graph, a periodic schedule, an imperative step function, C or assembly, HDL, or a simulator plus metadata. An execution trace is evidence from one run, not generally the compiled program. Likewise, generated HDL is still input to synthesis, while a mapped netlist is not yet placement and routing. The survey by Johnston and colleagues describes early dataflow graphs as machine-level representations, but that historical implementation choice is not the definition of dataflow \cite{johnston2004dataflow}.

\section{Compare Two Tiny Specifications}

Lucid equations denote streams; the order of equations does not prescribe a schedule. A small fragment is:

\begin{verbatim}
out
where
  count = 0 fby count + 1;
  out = input + count;
end
\end{verbatim}

An actor-network notation can instead expose channels and firings:

\begin{verbatim}
actor AddOne in (x) out (y)
  action x:[v] ==> y:[v + 1] end
end
\end{verbatim}

The Lucid implementation may demand only selected elements of \texttt{out}; an actor implementation may repeatedly consume and produce tokens. Neither syntax requires one operating-system thread per equation or actor. \Cref{fig:visual-352} shows the distinct handoffs that prevent an implementation artifact from being mistaken for source semantics.

