% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Lower Through an Explicit Contract}

\begin{verbatim}
model := elaborate(source)
check_types_and_effects(model)
ir := graph(nodes=operators, edges=dependencies)
annotate(ir, state, clocks_or_rates, source_provenance)
if statically_schedulable(ir):
    artifact := emit_step_function(schedule(ir))
else:
    artifact := emit_runtime_network(ir)
record_manifest(artifact, semantics, target, assumptions)
\end{verbatim}

The semantic IR needs operator nodes and dependency or channel edges; explicit delay or actor-local state; clock or token-rate annotations when the model has them; value types and effects such as I/O or possible failure; and provenance from each node to source equations and locations. Compilation produces the selected artifact plus a manifest stating its interface and assumptions. A graph without this information may be useful for visualization but is too ambiguous to justify code generation.

\section{Optimize Only Within the Chosen Observations}

Legal optimizations include common-subexpression elimination for pure, same-context nodes and rescheduling independent nodes. Actor fusion can remove queues when it preserves channel order, blocking behavior visible at the boundary, and required boundedness. Clock-based dead-code elimination may remove an equation never present on an observable clock. These are conditional permissions, not consequences of drawing arrows.

Semantic-preservation risks include duplicating an effectful node, changing floating-point association, replacing an unbounded semantic channel with a bounded queue that deadlocks, or evaluating a previously undemanded partial expression. Test the emitted artifact against the source observation appropriate to the model---stream prefixes, reaction traces, channel histories, or returned values---rather than merely comparing internal schedules.

\section{Choose an Oracle Before Emitting Code}

Extend the two sample specifications to run on input \(3,5,10\). The stream equation yields \(3,6,12\); the actor yields \(4,6,11\). Agreement on the middle item is accidental. A compiler can choose the same host representation for both outputs, such as a vector, but the tests must still be derived independently from each specification. The stream prefix includes an index-dependent contribution and the actor observation includes how many input tokens were consumed. A test that checks only final length would accept switching the two implementations.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Model & Oracle input & Observation to retain \\
\hline
Indexed stream & Ordered demand keys & Value or error per key \\
FIFO actor & Token arrivals & Output and consumed prefix \\
Clocked node & Presence/value trace & Presence and value per instant \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
verify_fixture(source, compiled, fixture):
    oracle := new_reference_instance(source)
    target := new_compiled_instance(compiled)
    for input_event in fixture:
        expected := oracle.observe(input_event)
        actual := target.observe(input_event)
        require equivalent_at_boundary(expected, actual)
    require oracle.pending_requests() == target.pending_requests()
\end{verbatim}

An observation can be a batch rather than a single result. For a deterministic unit-rate actor, compare output tokens after quiescing the fixture, along with the unread input. For an indexed stream, query indices out of order, then repeat one. For a clocked node, place an absent instant between the first two samples: the absent instant is not interchangeable with a sample whose value is zero. Record each fixture's observation boundary so a test harness does not accidentally require the target to reproduce the interpreter's internal scheduling. The following chapters explain how each choice of observation constrains the compiler.
